-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I have just uploaded Yard version 1.5 to Sunsite.  Yard is a suite of Perl
scripts for creating and checking custom rescue disks (also called
bootdisks, boot floppies, etc).  Yard exploits the compressed ramdisk
feature of 1.3.48+ kernels, and requires Perl 5.

Its location at the moment is :
	ftp://sunsite.unc.edu/pub/Linux/Incoming/yard-1.5.tar.gz

Its eventual home will be in /pub/Linux/system/Recovery


New with 1.5:

- - LILO is no longer required.  If configuration variable $use_lilo == 0,
  write_rescue_disk will write kernel directly without LILO.
- - The configuration variables $lilo_compact and $lilo_append are obsolete.
  "make copies" now creates an initial ./Replacements/etc/lilo.conf, which
  can be edited.  This is more convenient and more flexible.
- - write_rescue_disk offers to make a double-disk set if one isn't enough.
- - write_rescue_disk checks floppy drive for writeable disk.
- - write_rescue_disk puts a sentinel boot sector on the root floppy.
- - Additions to Bootdisk_Contents.in and Replacements/etc/rc for modules.
- - Replacement specifications "file1 <= file2" now search PATH for file2.
- - $yard_temp is now a configuration variable rather than an environment
  variable.
- - Scripts are directly executable and no longer have .pl extensions
- - Configuration variables are now in CFG package.
- - Extensive code tweaks to satisfy my aesthetic whims.
Bugs fixed:
	check_root_fs created OLDROOT mount points for swap partitions
	check_root_fs complained about NFS fstab entries
	include_file tried to dereference links into /proc
	bugs with space calculations
        Yard assumed any executable binary file was loadable (a.out or ELF)
	bug in Perl 5.001n circumvented



