-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Here is a first draft of a Linux 2.0 + Quotas mini-Howto. I knocked
this together because there doesn't seem to be much docs on quotas
and there is a reasonable amount of newsgroup traffic looking for 
info on setting it up ... 

Comments, criticisms and contributions to "pcor@eek.ucg.ie" ...

- ----------------------------------------------------------------------

                Quotas + Linux 2.0 mini- Howto
               Peter Corcoran <pcor@eek.ucg.ie>
                            Jul 1996

  1. Why this HOWTO?

  Getting Quotas to work with Linux isn't very difficult, but there 
  isn't much by way of docs available to tell you how to do it.
  I also noticed a reasonable of newsgroup traffic from people asking 
  about Quotas, but most of the replies tend to be quite terse.

  This is intended as a first-pass at a mini-Howto on this topic - I 
  would invite additional contributions as my own experience is limited
  to RedHat and kernel version 2.0.x. If you have any contributions mail
  them to me at the above address.

  This document will outline:
  
  * What you need to run Quotas for kernel versions 2.0.x.
  * Where to get the Quotas software; how to install it.
  * A summary of what commands you've installed and how to use them.
  * Some simple examples of using Quotas.

 
  2. Requirements
	
  There are three main steps to get Quotas working. Firstly you need the
  2.0 kernel. Secondly you need the Quotas package. Thirdly you need to 
  do some basic system-level configuration. Lets take these in turn:


  2.1 The Kernel

  I will assme you have kernel 2.0.x running on your machine. If you have
  got RedHat 3.0.3 them check out the upgrade proceedure at their Web site.
  When upgrading the rpms don't do "rpm -Uvh *.rpm" as RedHat advise - you 
  need to upgrade the "initscripts" rpm first or some of the others won't 
  install. If you haven't got RedHat but have kernel 2.0.x then most of this
  will still apply. It should also work with 1.3.xx kernels, but I think you
  have to get some patches for some of the earlier 1.3 series. Mail me if you 
  have experience with this.

  The next step is to compile a kernel with Quotas support. I assume you've 
  already compiled a suitable kernel for your machine; if you've also added in
  support for Quotas you are done and can skip to the next step. If not, go into
  /usr/src/linux and do a "make config". In the latter stages of the configuration
  proceedure you will be asked if you want Quotas support - say yes. Do the usual
  make clean ; make dep ; make zImage, or whatever and get a cup of coffee while 
  you wait. If everything goes OK copy the new image to "vmlinuz.new"; RUN LILO 
  and reboot - you are done with step I.
  compiles cleanly you're finished with step I; 


  2.2 The Quotas Package
  
  The second thing you need is the Quotas package. If you've got RedHat this bit 
  is easy - go to "ftp.redhat.com/pub/contrib/RPMS" and download the basic "quota-
  1.xx-x.i386.rpm" package. As of writing the current version is 1.52-3. Now do 
  the usual "rpm -ivh quota-1.52-3.i386.rpm" and everything should be dandy. You 
  can skip to step III.

  If you don't have RedHat you may be able to install using rpm. If you can, do it
  because the non-rpm version of Quotas is a bit short on docs and doesn't conpile
  unless you mess around with .h files. If you want the source version go to sunsite
  or your local mirror and look in the "system/admin" directory where you should find
  "quota-1.52-3.tgz" or whatever. There may also be an older "quota+acct" package -
  this is quite old and I don't know if it is any use, but it does have better docs
  than the source distribution of Quotas. Now I couldn't get the source distribution 
  to compile, but I have seen newsgroup postings advising that you must copy the 
  "maintent.h" file into "/usr/src/include" in order to get it to compile. I have a 
  "maintent.h" file already in "/usr/src/include" which is substantially different 
  from the one included with the Quotas source distribution so if you do take this 
  approach I would suggest backing up your old "maintent.h" file. 

  
  2.3 Configuring Quotas
  
  If you've got past steps I & II you should now have a Quota-enabled kernel and a 
  set of binaries for manipulating, editing and tracking user and group quotas. The
  binaries from quotas live in "/usr/sbin" and you should also have man pages installed
  so that you can really get into the nitty-gritty if you want. I'll list the binaries
  in section 3, however, if you just want to get things running you'll need to do a 
  little bit-een (Irish colloquialism, sorry ;-) of basic system configuration.

  First you want to tell Quotas which disk partitions have quota checking enabled on 
  them. You do this in "/etc/fstab" with something like:

	/dev/hda4	/home 	ext2	defaults,usrquota	1 2 

  which turns on user level quota checking on my home partition. Naturally you will 
  have to unmount and remount your partition to enable Quotas. But read the next bit 
  first, because its probably easier to make all the changes and then reboot. 
  Incidently if you wanted grouplevel quotas as well you'd have "defaults,usrquota,
  grpquota" in the above.  

  Next you want Quotas to start up when you boot the system. So go to your "/etc/rc.d"
  directory and insert in your "rc.local" script the following (if you're using some
  other distribution you may not have an rc.local):
	
	/usr/sbin/quotacheck -av[ug]
	/usr/sbin/quotaon -av[ug]
  
  Now "quotacheck" starts checking user/group quotas against actual file usage; if you
  don't start it up you will find that the Quotas system doesn't update file usage 
  which can be problematic ;-). And I'm sure you can guess what "quotaon" does ;-).
  The -a option selects all active disk partitions with Quota-enabled filesystems; the 
  -v is for verbose mode. The -u option is user-level quota checking (default); the -g
  selects group level checking. 
 
     
  3. Whats in Quotas

  This is a brief summary of what is in the Quotas package - it probably isn't 
  complete, but these are the bits I've figured out. Let me know if there is other
  stuff that should go in here. Also this is terse - read the man pages for details.

	edquota -[avug]     	Edit a user's quota; starts a vi session to set a
				new quota or edit existing user quotas; data is 
				kept in "/partition/quota.user" or "/partition/quota.grp"

	quotaon -[avug]		Turn on quotas for all [-a] or for a particular user 
				or group; [-v] for verbose mode.

	quotaoff -[avug]	Turn off quotas for all [-a] or for a particular user 
				or group; [-v] for verbose mode.

	quotacheck -[avug]	Enables checking of user/group quotas. If you don't do 
				this you will get some very funny results; file usage
				won't stay updated, etc ...

	repquota -[avug]	Reporting on Quotas by users/groups. This is the easiest
				way for a sysadmin to keep a check on who is doing what.


  4. Some Quick 'n Dirty Examples	
  
  You should have enough in the above to get started, but its always nice to see how 
  someone else has done it so I'm going to throw in a couple of examples from my own
  setup to get you started:

  I've set up Quotas only on my home partition. Here is a typical listing from 
  "repquota":

	                        Block limits               File limits
	User            used    soft    hard  grace    used  soft  hard  grace
	root      --      14       0       0              0     0     0       
	bin       --      12       0       0              1     0     0       
	pcor      --   11006   20000   25000             73   100   120       
	joe       --    5843   10000   12000             23    50    60       
	kev       --      33   10000   12000             26    50    60       

  So I've given a typical user a "soft" quota of 10 MB, and a "hard" limit of 12 MB. 
  They get "soft" and "hard" file limits of 50 and 60 each. I don't know how practical 
  these limits are, as this is a new server I'm in the process of comissioning; comments
  appreciated.


  Here is what my "/etc/rc.d/rc.local" looks like:
	
	#!/bin/sh

	# This script will be executed *after* all the other init scripts.
	# You can put your own initialization stuff in here if you don't
	# want to do the full Sys V style init stuff.

	if [ -f /etc/redhat-release ]; then
	R=$(cat /etc/redhat-release)
	else
		R="release 3.0.3"
	fi

	arch=$(uname -a)
	a="a"
	case "_$arch" in
        _a*) a="an";;
        _i*) a="an";;
	esac

	/usr/sbin/quotacheck -av
	/usr/sbin/quotaon -av

	# This will overwrite /etc/issue at every boot.  So, make any changes you
	# want to make to /etc/issue here or you will lose them when you reboot.
	echo "" > /etc/issue
	echo "WuzWuz running Red Hat Linux $R" >> /etc/issue
	echo "Kernel $(uname -r) on $a $(uname -m)" >> /etc/issue

	cp -f /etc/issue /etc/issue.net
	echo >> /etc/issue

  Note the order of "quotacheck" and "quotaon" - this is, apparently, quite important.
  Finally note my fstab:

	#
	# /etc/fstab
	#
	# You should be using fstool (in the control-panel) to edit this!
	#
	
	/dev/hda1           /                   ext2      defaults   1 1
	/dev/hda4           /home               ext2      defaults,usrquota   1 2
	/dev/hdb4           /home/httpd         ext2      defaults   1 2
	/dev/hdb2           /home/samba         ext2      defaults   1 2
	/dev/hda3           /usr                ext2      defaults   1 2
	/dev/hdb3           /usr/nfs/usr        ext2      defaults   1 2
	/dev/hda2           /var                ext2      defaults   1 2

	#
	# proc filesystem
	#

	/proc               /proc               proc      defaults   0 0
	/dev/fd0            /mnt/floppy         ext2      defaults,noauto 0 0
	/dev/cdrom          /mnt/cdrom          iso9660   ro,noauto  0 0

	#
	# Swap partitions
	#

	/dev/hdb1                      none            swap   sw
  
  Well, thats about it. As I indicated above this is a first pass at a Quotas mini-Howto.
  It is currently RedHat centric, because my experience only covers RedHat. Naturally
  I would welcome contributions, comments, and constructive criticism. I threw this 
  together in a hurry, because I  couldn't find much by way of docs on Quotas and had to
  figure most of this out myself. Hopefully you will find it useful - let me know. Also
  if you want to contribute/co-author/maintain this document let me know. 

  Peter Corcoran, Galway, Ireland, 15/7/96.

	


