Hi

 I recently uploaded version 2.44 of my compiler compiler to sunsite.
The lsm entry is below. I guess it'll go to devel/lang in the end.

I put a lot of work into the gnu autoconf so please have a go and tell
me in what way it blows up. I didn't have the heart to try it on NetBSD
one last time, but I know it works on Linux because that's where I do
the development. Also on SunOS and Slo-aris, because that's what I'm
talking to you through. HP's are known to be roughly OK too. I need
data on alpha's.

PRECC is an infinite lookahead compiler compiler. People tend to like
that, but the actual neat trick is the parameterized clauses and
synthesis of attributes on the fly, with backtracking.  You can
also parameterize on other clauses (it's higher order). Fully compatible
with [f]lex, should you use that underneath.

Regards

Peter T. Breuer


Title:		PRECCX
Version:	2.44
Entered-date:	12jul96
Description:	PRECCX is an infinite-lookahead compiler-compiler for
                context-dependent attributed grammars. It uses and
                produces ANSI C and is plug-compatible with lex/flex
                lexers.
                Compiler-compilers aren't that easy to describe! Cf.
                yacc or bison. 
                This is the third public and fifth overall release
                in the 2.4x line begun in 1993. It is the production
                release of the 2.43 development code.  The changes
                since 2.42 have been aimed mainly at portability.
                If you wonder what the difference is with respect to
                yacc and friends, it is: parameterized grammar clauses,
                higher order "macros", arbitrary lookahead (yacc has
                1-token lookahead), referential transparancy, ... in
                summary, it's just not the same technology.
                Substantial examples included.
