One of the things that hamper Linux's climb to world domination 
is the shortage of bad Computer Role Playing Games, or CRaPGs. No 
operating system can be considered respectable without one.
	I have accordingly spared little trouble and almost no expense 
to port from the Atari ST 'Anoraks of Doom', which has many of the less
attractive features of 'Bard's Tale I' and the early Ultimas, with a
hexagonal grid pattern that most RPG designers have wisely eschewed.
	For those of you who do not read the quality press, ST Format
awarded it 83% and described it as 'Delightful', but it was a quiet
month on the normally hectic Atari scene.
	The second part of the Anchovy Trilogy, 'The Towers of Saucisse-
Notah', is currently undergoing beta-testing; its uplifting quality can
be gauged from the fact that one of the testers has sold his equipment
and joined a monastery. 'Pizza Reaction', the triumphant conclusion,
will be written as soon as I can think of a plot.
	The game, anoraks.2.0.0.tgz, will be available from sunsite 
until somebody responsible notices it and deletes it, and shortly from 
ftp.mee.tcd.ie/pub/Brian, though they don't know that yet.
	Unless I am stopped, I will soon have finished porting 'HexAID',
the kit I made for producing these CRaPGs, and I will upload that to the
same sites. You have been warned.
	Apologies for the waste of bandwidth.
Brian O'Donnell
odonnllb@tcd.ie


